This page sits inside Grothendieck's long computation, in 'variations sur l'équation des lacets', of a Grothendieck–Teichmüller-type group attached to a profinite group Ĝ^+. Ĝ^+ is plausibly the profinite completion of (P)SL2(Z) ≅ Z/2 * Z/3, with ρ and σ torsion generators. The ingredients are: τ' (an outer involution, complex-conjugation type, sending ρ to ρ^{-1}); normalisers N_ρ, N_σ; centralisers Z_ρ, Z_σ; a character χ to Ẑ*; and an element ε_0 with u(ε_0) = ε_0^μ.

The previous page defined the étale part Σ^ét_{ρ,σ} of the set Σ_{ρ,σ} of 'commutator data' (ξ, η, μ, ε', ε). It identified Σ^ét_{ρ,σ} bijectively, via (73), with a subgroup Z_{ρ,σ} (also written B_{ρ,σ}) of Aut(Ĝ^+) × Ẑ* × {±1} × {±1}. This consists of automorphisms u sending ρ to a conjugate of ρ^{ε'} and σ to a conjugate of σ^{ε}, with u(ε_0) = ε_0^μ. It also defined SG^ét_{ρ,σ} as the set of solutions (α, β, μ) ∈ Ĝ^+ × Ĝ^+ × Ẑ* of the loop equation [α,ρ] = [β,σ]·ε_1^{μ-1}, subject to the existence of u with u(ρ) = α(ρ) and u(σ) = β(σ).

On this page Grothendieck introduces the fibre product S_0Γ_{ρ,σ} = Z_{ρ,σ} ×_γ N*_ρ ×_γ N*_σ, whose elements are triples x = (u, a, b) with a, b normalising elements matched with u. Equation (76) defines the canonical map x ↦ (α, β, μ):
- α = u·i_ρ(a)^{-1}·τ'^{ν'(x)};
- β = u·i_σ(b)^{-1}·τ'^{ν(x)};
- μ = χ(x);
- here (-1)^{ν'} = ε' and (-1)^{ν} = ε.

The fibre-product condition ensures u·a^{-1} and u·b^{-1} are inner (in Ĝ^+, up to τ'). Hence α and β define elements of the group, and (77) holds: α(ρ) = u(a^{-1}(ρ^{ε'})) = u(ρ), and β(σ) = u(σ). Therefore [α,ρ] = [u,ρ] and [β,σ] = [u,σ]. The loop equation [α,ρ] = [β,σ]·ε_1^{μ-1} then becomes exactly the defining condition u(ε_0) = ε_0^μ of Z_{ρ,σ}. So the image lies in SG^ét.

Injectivity is argued as follows. By (78), ν'(x) and ν(x) are read off as the parities ν(α) and ν(β), and μ = χ(u). One then recovers a = τ'^{ν(α)}·α^{-1}·u and b = τ'^{ν(β)}·β^{-1}·u, so it suffices to recover u. Since u ∈ Z_{ρ,σ}, which embeds via its Aut(Ĝ^+) component, u is determined by u(ρ) = α(ρ) and u(σ) = β(σ) through (77), ρ and σ being generators.

The next page proves surjectivity. It uses the free action of SN*_ρ × SN*_σ = SZ_ρ × SZ_σ on SG_{ρ,σ}, whose quotient is Σ_{ρ,σ}. The upshot is an identification of the group-theoretic object S_0Γ_{ρ,σ} (automorphisms plus normaliser data) with the solution set of the loop equation. This is a GT-style description of automorphisms of Ĝ^+ by pairs (α, β, μ) satisfying a commutator equation.
